Found 2026-08-28 by an independent cross-check (Codex, read-only) of the
k79 type model, reproduced by the orchestrating agent before filing. Three
defects and one under-tested claim, all in lib/API/Docker/Role/Type.pm and
lib/API/Docker/Type.pm.

1. BUILDARGS RESOLVES THE PERL NAME FIRST, WHICH BREAKS INVARIANT 2.

   ContainerInspectResponse->from_data({ id => "lowercase-new-field" })->TO_JSON
   -> {"Id":"lowercase-new-field"}          measured
      unknown_fields                        {}

   The model has never heard of a wire field `id`, but reads it as the Perl
   spelling of the known wire field `Id` and rewrites the key. The invariant
   says an unrecognised field goes in and comes out unchanged.

   from_data's own POD already says the right thing -- "matched against the
   registry's wire names first and the Perl attribute names second" -- and the
   shared BUILDARGS does the opposite. The POD is right, the code is wrong.

   Reach, measured over all 201 classes: 955 attributes have a Perl name that
   differs from their wire name, so 955 keys would be rewritten if a daemon
   sent them in that spelling. Real collisions today (a Perl name that is
   another field's wire name in the same class): ZERO. But the swagger mixes
   cases freely -- 114 fields have a lowercase wire name, BuildInfo.id among
   them -- so a lowercase daemon key is normal, not exotic.

   Fix shape (Codex's, and it is better than treating both the same): the two
   entry points have different jobs. `from_data` inflates an engine RESPONSE
   and resolves wire-first, preserving what it does not know. `new` builds a
   REQUEST from caller-authored data and may keep Perl-first with wire
   aliases. Today from_data is a thin wrapper around new, which makes the
   distinction impossible.

2. AN AMBIGUOUS CONSTRUCTOR PICKS NONDETERMINISTICALLY.

   HostConfig->new(privileged => 0, Privileged => 1)

   Both spellings land on the same attribute and hash order decides. Measured
   over 20 constructions in one process: 0 came out 9 times, 1 came out 11.
   It should be rejected as ambiguous, even when both values agree.

   t/type.t:245 cannot catch it -- it builds one object per spelling and never
   passes both together.

3. THE DSL DOES NOT REJECT A DUPLICATE `wire` VALUE.

   _docker rejects a duplicate Perl attribute name but not a duplicate wire
   name, and _docker_wire_index is last-one-wins. Two fields declared with
   wire => 'CPUShares' would make one unreachable on inflation and let
   TO_JSON write both to one key in attribute order. Nothing in the 201
   generated classes does this today; the generator could produce it tomorrow.
   A guard belongs where the Perl-name guard already is.

4. UNKNOWN_FIELDS LOSES TO A KNOWN FIELD, SILENTLY.

   HostConfig->new(unknown_fields => { Privileged => "engine-value" },
                   privileged => 0)->TO_JSON
   -> {"Privileged":false}                  measured, the raw value is gone

   Defensible as long as unknown_fields only ever holds names the registry
   does not know. It stops being defensible under k83 option (b), which would
   deliberately put a KNOWN wire name in there after a type mismatch -- the
   preserved engine value would then be overwritten by the empty typed one,
   and "nothing is lost" would be false. Decide the precedence when k83 is
   decided, or give rejected values their own store.

5. TEST GAPS in t/type.t (the tests hold for what they cover; the claim is
   broader than the coverage):
     - the caller-data-key invariant names ten fields and exercises six.
       ExposedPorts, Volumes, DriverOpts and Options are not pinned, so a
       generator regression that misdeclares one of those four passes.
     - no test constructs both spellings of one field together (defect 2).
     - no test pins two-pass BUILDARGS idempotence with unknown_fields
       already populated (the allOf shape composes the role into both
       classes, so the modifier runs twice).

Lane: api-docker-type-writer. Defect 1 interacts with k83 -- decide k83
first or together, since both turn on what from_data does with a value it
cannot use.

Defects 1, 2, 3 and 5 fixed in dc59680, each reproduced red first. Defect 4 answered rather than fixed: no collision can arise out of from_data, because a value lands in unknown_fields under a known wire name only when the typed attribute is unset, and TO_JSON skips unset attributes -- and defect 3's new guard makes wire-name uniqueness structural rather than incidental. Pinned by round-trip assertions instead of a precedence rule. Verified independently: prove 39/455 PASS, live against Podman 39/434 PASS, --verify 201 identical, drift 0 in all seven tiers, sibling repo 16/103 PASS with PERL5LIB pointed at this working tree.
